,0.032,0.073,0.051,0.041,0.069,0.038,0.063,0.032,0.072,0.034,0.027,0.02,0.045,0.034,0.022,0.062,0.031,0.085,0.09,0.054,0.065,0.109,0.057,0.052,0.048,0.012,0.013,0.036,0.011,0.011,0.077,0.093,0.034,0.009,0.019,0.01,0.035,0.05,0.025,0.009,0.016,0.027,0.021,0.023,0.024,0.036,0.02,0.075,0.1,0.081,0.021,0.046,0.027,0.035,0.01,0.005,0.01,0.008,0.021,0.015,0.127,0.033,0.017,0.035,0.064,0.118,0.006,0.064,0.048,0.028,0.026,0.032,0.028,0.015,0.006,0.029,0,0.015,0.035,0.019,0.033,0.019,0.023,0.018,0.02,0.023,0.055,0.01,0.01,0.001,0.022,0.04,0.055,0.023,0.033,0.019,0.031,0.034,0.023,0.047,0.012,0.038,0.018,0.033,0.033,0.013,0.012,0.044,0.103,0.033,0.003,0.017,0.023,0.017,0.041,0.032,0.047,0.043,0.017,0.031,0.029,0.027,0.047,0.023,0.005,0.031,0.035,0.011,0.049,0.047,0.029,0.033]}},"alerts":[{"event":"Flash Flood Warning","headline":"Flash Flood Warning issued August 16 at 2:35PM MDT until August 16 at 4:45PM MDT by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"...FLASH FLOOD WARNING FOR THE ASPEN ACRES BURN SCAR REMAINS IN\nEFFECT UNTIL 445 PM MDT THIS AFTERNOON FOR EASTERN CUSTER AND\nSOUTHWESTERN PUEBLO COUNTIES...\n\nAt 235 PM MDT, Emergency management reported flash flooding over the\nAspen Acres Burn Scar. Between 1 and 1.5 inches of rain have fallen.\nFlash flooding is already occurring. Excessive rainfall over the burn\nscar will result in debris flow. The debris flow can consist of rock,\nmud, vegetation and other loose materials.\n\nFLASH FLOOD EMERGENCY for Middle, Squirrel Creek and North St\nCharles River through Beulah. This is a PARTICULARLY DANGEROUS\nSITUATION. SEEK HIGHER GROUND NOW!\n\nHAZARD...Life threatening flash flooding. Heavy rain producing flash\nflooding in and around the Aspen Acres Burn Scar.\n\nSOURCE...Emergency management reported.\n\nIMPACT...This is a PARTICULARLY DANGEROUS SITUATION. SEEK HIGHER\nGROUND NOW! IMMEDIATE EVACUATION along waterways and flood\nprone areas in and around the Aspen Acres Burn Scar.  Climb\nto higher ground if flood water threatens!\n\nSome locations that will experience flash flooding include...\nBeulah.\n\nThis includes the following high risk locations...\nRoads and residents along the Middle, Squirrel and North Creek as\nwell as the North St Charles River in the Beulah area.","ends":"2026-08-16T16:45:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Custer, CO; Pueblo, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.6b9b0bd826c3ddf00b19efb4187ee9308f42ea43.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.6b9b0bd826c3ddf00b19efb4187ee9308f42ea43.001.1"},{"event":"Flash Flood Warning","headline":"Flash Flood Warning issued August 16 at 1:12PM MDT until August 16 at 4:15PM MDT by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"FFWPUB\n\nThe National Weather Service in Pueblo has issued a\n\n* Flash Flood Warning for...\nThe Middle Aspen Acres Burn Scar in...\nEast Central Custer County in southeastern Colorado...\nWest Central Pueblo County in southeastern Colorado...\n\n* Until 415 PM MDT.\n\n* At 112 PM MDT, Doppler radar indicated thunderstorms producing\nheavy rain over the Middle Aspen Acres Burn Scar. Up to 0.5 inches\nof rain have fallen. The expected rainfall rate is 1.5 to 2.5\ninches in 1 hour. Additional rainfall amounts of 0.5 to 1 inch are\npossible in the warned area. Flash flooding is ongoing or expected\nto begin shortly. Excessive rainfall over the burn scar will result\nin debris flow moving through the North Saint Charles, South Creek,\nSquirrel Creek, Middle Creek, and North Creek Drainages. The debris\nflow can consist of rock, mud, vegetation and other loose\nmaterials.\n\nHAZARD...Life threatening flash flooding. Thunderstorms producing\nflash flooding in and around the Middle Aspen Acres Burn\nScar.\n\nSOURCE...Radar indicated.\n\nIMPACT...Life threatening flash flooding of areas in and around the\nMiddle Aspen Acres Burn Scar.\n\n* Some locations that will experience flash flooding include...\nSan Isabel, Highway 165, Beulah, Highway 78, stream crossings, and\ncounty roads within and immediately downstream of the burn scar.\n\nThis includes the following high risk locations...\nBeulah Water Intake, Northcreek Road county line to Red Mountain Camp\nand Northcreek Road from Red Mountain Camp to Beulah.","ends":"2026-08-16T16:15:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Custer, CO; Pueblo, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.01352e3de1b1bc7a9f5d24105d1d28351e2c0da4.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.01352e3de1b1bc7a9f5d24105d1d28351e2c0da4.001.1"},{"event":"Flash Flood Watch","headline":"Flash Flood Watch issued August 16 at 11:50AM MDT until August 16 at 8:00PM MDT by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"* WHAT...Flash flooding and debris flows caused by excessive rainfall\ncontinue to be possible over the Aspen Acres burn scar.\n\n* WHERE...Aspen Acres Burn Scar\n\n* WHEN...Until 8 PM MDT this evening.\n\n* IMPACTS...Heavy rainfall over the Aspen Acres burn scar is expected\nup to and during the period of the watch. Residents near the Aspen\nAcres burn scar should prepare for potential debris flow. Be sure\nto stay up to date with information from local authorities. Heavy\nrainfall could trigger flash flooding of low-lying areas, street\nflooding, and debris flows in and near recent wildfire burn scars.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- National Weather Service Meteorologists are forecasting\nthunderstorms with heavy rainfall over the burn scar, which may\nlead to flash flooding and debris flows.\n- Some locations that may experience flash flooding include...\nBeulah, San Isabel, and highways 165 and 78 in and around the\nAspen Acres Burn Scar.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-08-16T20:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Wet Mountains between 6300 and 10000Ft; Wet Mountains above 10000 Ft; Pueblo Vicinity/Pueblo County Below 6300 Feet","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.6093b695973dc511b011f9be31951334c8aca69a.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.6093b695973dc511b011f9be31951334c8ac